# ── Runtime ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
PORT=3001
API_PREFIX=api/v1
LOG_LEVEL=info

# ── URLs ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# APP_URL is the Next.js frontend; BETTER_AUTH_URL is this API's public origin.
APP_URL=http://localhost:3000
BETTER_AUTH_URL=http://localhost:3001
# Comma-separated. REQUIRED in production, and must not contain localhost —
# session cookies make CORS an authentication control, not a convenience.
ALLOWED_ORIGINS=http://localhost:3000
# The parent domain the session cookie is written for, leading dot included.
# Set it when the API and the web app are on sibling subdomains, which is the
# deployed shape: the web app reads this cookie itself, so a cookie scoped to
# the API's host alone leaves every protected page redirecting to sign-in.
# Leave empty for local development.
COOKIE_DOMAIN=

# ── Neon PostgreSQL (server-only — never expose these) ────────────────────
# DATABASE_URL: the POOLED endpoint (host contains `-pooler`). Used at runtime.
# DIRECT_DATABASE_URL: the DIRECT endpoint. Migrations only — DDL cannot run
# through PgBouncer in transaction mode.
# Neon Console → your project → Connection Details.
DATABASE_URL=
DIRECT_DATABASE_URL=

# ── Auth ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# At least 32 characters. Generate with: openssl rand -base64 48
BETTER_AUTH_SECRET=

# ── AI (server-only — an exposed key is a billable incident) ──────────────
# Which provider generates dishes. The engine reuses existing recipes before it
# generates anything, so most plans make no call at all — see
# docs/decisions/0006-reuse-before-generating.md.
#
#   stub      — no provider, no key, no spend. Plans are built purely from recipes
#               already in the library; generation fails honestly if that is not
#               enough. This is the default, and a valid way to run.
#   google    — free tier, no card. Key: aistudio.google.com/apikey
#               Model availability changes: a key issued today may not be able to
#               use an older model. Leave AI_MODEL empty and the default follows.
#               Check their terms on training from submitted data before sending
#               anything health-adjacent.
#   anthropic — paid. Key: console.anthropic.com (needs a card)
#   ollama    — a model on your own machine. No key; set AI_BASE_URL.
#   omniroute — an OpenAI-compatible gateway (e.g. OmniRoute) holding the
#               vendor keys itself. Set AI_BASE_URL (default
#               http://localhost:20128/v1), OMNIROUTE_API_KEY and
#               OMNIROUTE_MODEL. The model decides which account a call
#               spends against: gemini/* still reaches Google through it.
#   openrouter — OpenRouter directly, on paid models that never train on what
#               they are sent (0064). Key: openrouter.ai/settings/keys, on an
#               account with training off and zero data retention on; every
#               request also carries provider.zdr and data_collection: deny.
#               Set OPENROUTER_API_KEY, and AI_FALLBACK_MODELS and
#               AI_REASONING_EFFORT below. Leave AI_BASE_URL empty.
AI_PROVIDER=stub

# LEAVE THIS EMPTY unless you want a specific model. Empty means "the chosen
# provider's default": claude-sonnet-5, gemini-3.6-flash, llama3.1,
# NutrIA-Fallback for omniroute, or deepseek/deepseek-v4.1-flash for openrouter.
#
# Model names are provider-specific, so a value left over from another provider
# is rejected at boot rather than failing on the first generation.
AI_MODEL=

# What your provider allows you, as its console reports it — so /admin can say how
# close today is to the wall (0035).
#
# Yours, not ours: Gemini's free tier gives one model 20 requests a day and another
# 500, and the number that bites is usually requests rather than tokens. Leave them
# empty and the panel shows the count with no bar, which is honest — a limit nobody
# stated is not one this product may invent.
#
# It counts what leaves this service. If the provider's console says something else,
# the difference is calls that did not come through here.
AI_REQUESTS_PER_DAY=
AI_TOKENS_PER_MINUTE=

# Only the key for the selected provider is required; the others may stay empty.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=
GOOGLE_API_KEY=
OMNIROUTE_API_KEY=
# omniroute only: the gateway alias or routed model, e.g. NutrIA-Fallback or
# gemini/gemini-3.6-flash. Empty means NutrIA-Fallback. Wins over AI_MODEL.
OMNIROUTE_MODEL=
OPENROUTER_API_KEY=
# openrouter only: the models OpenRouter moves a request to, in order, when
# AI_MODEL is down, rate-limited or refuses — comma-separated vendor/model ids,
# never a :free one. Empty means AI_MODEL alone. 0064 chose minimax/minimax-m3.
AI_FALLBACK_MODELS=
# openrouter only: none, minimal, low, medium or high. Empty leaves the model's
# default. 0064 chose low: none answers faster and gets the grams wrong.
AI_REASONING_EFFORT=
# openrouter only: a cap on the thinking in tokens, a positive integer. When set
# it replaces AI_REASONING_EFFORT, except that none still switches thinking off.
AI_REASONING_MAX_TOKENS=
# openrouter only: throughput, latency or price — how OpenRouter orders the
# no-training endpoints. Empty keeps its load-balancing, measured ~3x slower
# under parallel requests. It never widens which endpoints may answer.
AI_PROVIDER_SORT=
# openrouter only: OpenRouter provider slugs it may never route to,
# comma-separated, e.g. sail-research (kept timing out). Empty ignores none.
# It only removes; the no-training rules still decide who may answer.
AI_PROVIDER_IGNORE=
# openrouter only, and REQUIRED with it (boot refuses without): the companies
# that may run the model, OpenRouter provider slugs, comma-separated. The
# privacy policy names them; zdr alone allowed 22 companies, one in Indonesia.
AI_PROVIDER_ONLY=deepinfra,coreweave
# openrouter only: the most a generation request may write per dish it asks
# for, in tokens — the cap is that times the dishes plus 800. Empty means 1200.
# Applied only with AI_REASONING_EFFORT=none or AI_REASONING_MAX_TOKENS set,
# since the thinking counts against the same tokens.
AI_MAX_OUTPUT_TOKENS_PER_DISH=

# ollama, omniroute and openrouter — the daemon or gateway's URL. Empty defaults
# to http://localhost:11434/api for ollama, http://localhost:20128/v1 for
# omniroute and https://openrouter.ai/api/v1 for openrouter. For openrouter it
# must be empty or on https://openrouter.ai, or the API refuses to boot: set to
# the gateway, it would send the OpenRouter key there. Switching from omniroute
# to openrouter, empty it.
AI_BASE_URL=

# How long, in seconds, the model half of a generation may take in all — every
# round of calls together. Empty means 170: what fits inside the 300-second
# function this API runs in on Vercel, with room left to schedule and save the
# plan. When it runs out, the library covers what the model did not bring, and
# the call log says the budget was spent. On a host with no such limit, raise it.
AI_BUDGET_SECONDS=

# ── Email ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Password-reset mail goes out over SMTP; any provider that speaks it will do
# (Gmail with an app password, or a transactional service on your own domain).
# All four are required together, or none: with SMTP_HOST empty, the reset link
# is written to the log so local development still works end to end.
# Verification links are never mailed — accounts are opened by hand (0017).
# Port 465 is implicit TLS; on anything else STARTTLS is required, and a server
# that does not offer it gets no mail rather than the password in cleartext.
EMAIL_FROM=
SMTP_HOST=
SMTP_PORT=587
SMTP_USER=
SMTP_PASS=

# Where "an account is waiting" goes while access opens by hand (0029). Unset,
# nobody is told about a sign-up and the owner queries the table instead.
OWNER_EMAIL=

# ── Push (0054) ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# The check-in reminder on the phones that ask for it, besides the mail. All
# three or none; none means mail only, and the profile offers no switch.
# Generate the pair once, and never commit the private half:
#   npx web-push generate-vapid-keys
# The subject is how a push service reaches the sender: mailto: or https:.
# Whether any reminder goes out at all is the switch on /admin (off by default).
VAPID_PUBLIC_KEY=
VAPID_PRIVATE_KEY=
VAPID_SUBJECT=

# ── Error reporting ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Unset means off: nothing is sent anywhere and the log is the only record.
# With a DSN, 500s and failed background jobs go to Sentry — the error, its
# stack and the route, never a body, a header, a cookie or a user (0024).
SENTRY_DSN=

# ── Limits ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
RATE_LIMIT_TTL=60
RATE_LIMIT_MAX=120

# Leave empty: on in development, off everywhere else. An explicit "true" is
# refused in production — a published schema maps the attack surface.
SWAGGER_ENABLED=

# ── Illustrations ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Off by default. The Google free tier allows zero image generations; enable
# billing on the Google AI project first, then set this to true. Each distinct
# recipe is drawn once (~4 cents), labelled as an AI illustration, stored in
# the database and served from one cached route. See docs/decisions/0010.
AI_ILLUSTRATIONS=false
# Off by default. On Google's free tier directly the rewrite sweep would spend
# the daily request cap generation needs in about two hours; through the
# gateway its free models carry it. The daily cron runs it (vercel.json).
AI_REWRITE_STEPS=false
# The model the rewrite sweep asks. Through the gateway: a combo of free models
# without the Gemini step, so a sweep never spends a plan's quota. Empty: the
# generation's model.
AI_REWRITE_MODEL=
# The platform sends it as a bearer token on cron calls (GET /cron/illustrate).
# Unset: the cron route answers 404 like everything else it will not admit to.
CRON_SECRET=


# ── Payments ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# All three unset means payments do not exist: nothing about them is reachable,
# and the tier is whatever the owner granted by hand (0042, and the step-by-step
# in docs/reference/payments.md). This is the shipped default.
#
# Stripe's TEST keys need nothing but an email address — no business
# verification, no bank account — and they exercise the whole flow with test
# cards. `sk_test_…` while building, `sk_live_…` only after the account is
# verified and the legal pages exist.
#
# Secret. It can move money, so it lives here and never crosses the network to
# a browser — the same rule as the AI keys above. The publishable key is not
# listed anywhere in this repository because this service has no use for it:
# Checkout is opened by redirecting to a URL the API asks Stripe for, so no card
# details and no Stripe SDK ever reach the page.
STRIPE_SECRET_KEY=

# Which price Checkout opens with (`price_…`). Configuration and not code,
# because the same build has to run against a test price and a live one.
STRIPE_PRICE_ID=

# Optional: a second, yearly price on the same product (`price_…`). With it the
# premium card offers monthly and yearly; without it, monthly alone. It needs
# the other three STRIPE_* values. The seven-day free trial needs nothing here.
STRIPE_YEARLY_PRICE_ID=

# Optional: the practice plans for professionals (0061), as price_…=N pairs,
# comma-separated — each a Stripe price and how many active clients it
# includes, e.g. price_practice_small=30,price_practice_large=60. A practice
# subscription on a price not listed here opens nothing. It needs the other
# three STRIPE_* values, and none of these may be a premium price. The 14-day
# practice trial needs nothing here.
STRIPE_PRACTICE_PRICES=

# The signing secret of the webhook endpoint (`whsec_…`), shown once by Stripe
# when the endpoint is created. It is the ONLY authority on a webhook: a browser
# returning from Checkout proves nothing, since anybody can request that URL.
# Unset, every webhook is refused — which is the correct failure.
STRIPE_WEBHOOK_SECRET=

# ─── Sign in with Google or Apple (0058) ─────────────────────────────────────
#
# Optional, and each provider is whole or absent: half of one is refused at
# boot. With none, the sign-in page is the form it always was.
#
# Google: an OAuth client ("Web application") from the Google Cloud console.
# Nothing to do with GOOGLE_API_KEY above, which is the AI provider's. Its one
# authorised redirect URI is
#   {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/{API_PREFIX}/auth/callback/google
# — the WEB origin in the deployed shape. Locally that is
#   http://localhost:3001/api/v1/auth/callback/google
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
GOOGLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_SECRET=

# Apple: needs the developer programme. The client id is the *Services ID*
# (not the app's bundle id), the key is the .p8 downloaded once — pasted as it
# is, or on one line with its line breaks written \n. The client secret Apple
# wants is a JWT signed from these four at boot, so nothing here expires.
# Return URL: {BETTER_AUTH_URL}/{API_PREFIX}/auth/callback/apple (https only).
APPLE_OAUTH_CLIENT_ID=
APPLE_OAUTH_TEAM_ID=
APPLE_OAUTH_KEY_ID=
APPLE_OAUTH_PRIVATE_KEY=
